"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,530\nEpisode 13\n\n3\n00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:10,125\nMotivation and reward\n\n4\n00:00:12,925 --> 00:00:15,848\nA monkey gets an unexpected sip of juice.\n\n5\n00:00:16,196 --> 00:00:19,689\nDopamine neurons in its midbrain fire a burst.\n\n6\n00:00:20,129 --> 00:00:22,929\nOnce a light reliably comes before the juice,\n\n7\n00:00:23,074 --> 00:00:24,705\nthe burst moves to the light.\n\n8\n00:00:25,217 --> 00:00:27,709\nAnd if the light comes but the juice does not,\n\n9\n00:00:27,873 --> 00:00:29,944\nthe neurons dip below baseline,\n\n10\n00:00:30,231 --> 00:00:31,847\nright when the juice was due.\n\n11\n00:00:32,017 --> 00:00:35,066\nThese neurons are not simply signaling pleasure.\n\n12\n00:00:35,445 --> 00:00:38,949\nThey track the difference between what was expected and what arrived,\n\n13\n00:00:39,052 --> 00:00:40,822\na reward prediction error.\n\n14\n00:00:41,241 --> 00:00:43,239\nBetter than expected, a burst.\n\n15\n00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:44,977\nAs expected, nothing.\n\n16\n00:00:45,309 --> 00:00:46,512\nWorse, a dip.\n\n17\n00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:49,664\nIt is the prediction signal from episode four,\n\n18\n00:00:49,684 --> 00:00:51,146\ntuned to what matters.\n\n19\n00:00:51,316 --> 00:00:53,784\nCuriosity recruits the same circuit.\n\n20\n00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:58,158\nPeople read trivia questions and waited a few seconds for each answer.\n\n21\n00:00:58,635 --> 00:01:00,203\nThe more curious they were,\n\n22\n00:01:00,431 --> 00:01:03,562\nthe more their midbrain reward areas engaged,\n\n23\n00:01:03,736 --> 00:01:05,733\nand the better they remembered the answer.\n\n24\n00:01:06,304 --> 00:01:08,861\nThey even remembered unrelated faces,\n\n25\n00:01:09,027 --> 00:01:10,298\nshown during that wait,\n\n26\n00:01:10,318 --> 00:01:11,166\na little better.\n\n27\n00:01:11,336 --> 00:01:13,364\nExternal rewards are trickier.\n\n28\n00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:18,541\nA meta-analysis of one hundred twenty-eight experiments found that expected,\n\n29\n00:01:18,748 --> 00:01:24,759\ntangible rewards for an interesting task reduced people's later free choice to keep doing it.\n\n30\n00:01:25,364 --> 00:01:28,823\nPositive, informative feedback did the opposite.\n\n31\n00:01:28,993 --> 00:01:30,130\nRelevance helps.\n\n32\n00:01:30,517 --> 00:01:38,237\nHigh school science students who wrote about how the material connected to their own lives gained interest and earned higher grades,\n\n33\n00:01:38,524 --> 00:01:41,373\nespecially those who had expected to do poorly.\n\n34\n00:01:41,543 --> 00:01:42,310\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:42,711 --> 00:01:45,086\nOpen with a question students want answered,\n\n36\n00:01:45,106 --> 00:01:46,794\nand pause before the reveal.\n\n37\n00:01:47,153 --> 00:01:48,911\nShow the gap before you fill it.\n\n38\n00:01:49,365 --> 00:01:51,047\nGive feedback that informs,\n\n39\n00:01:51,067 --> 00:01:52,978\nabout strategy and progress,\n\n40\n00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:54,786\nrather than points for everything.\n\n41\n00:01:55,333 --> 00:01:58,649\nAsk students to connect the material to their own goals.\n\n42\n00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:01,017\nAnd let surprise do some of the work.\n\n43\n00:02:01,187 --> 00:02:04,063\nThe brain learns most when it cares about the answer.\n\n44\n00:02:04,369 --> 00:02:05,725\nMake them want to know.\n\n"
